Date: Thu, 2 Dec 1993 16:37:53 +0500
From: brian@suneast.east.sun.com (Brian Hawthorne - SunSelect Strategic Marketing)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Charity for Entrepreneurs

> Agreed. The problem is even more difficult with "novel" start-ups
> like digital cash, hypertext, etc. Nobody really knows whether a
> market exists.

It is nearly impossible to create a market ex nihilo. For all of these
product ideas (digital cash, etc.), you need to define a new position
in peoples minds based on concepts they already have. Thus, store-and-
forward voice recording systems become "voice mail" and cellular radio-based
communication devices become "car phones".

The problem with "digital cash" is that it isn't cash in the way the
word is used by most people, and they don't really know what the digital
part means. For the concept to succeed, it needs to be packaged in a
familiar way (smart-cards, for example, that look like credit cards),
and related to something that people already are familiar with, such
as ATM cards or credit cards (see the recent proposal from Dave Kristol
at AT&T on the imp-interest list). 

You can also do research to see if the features of things like digital
cash actually result in benefits that people actually recognize, etc.
 
Figuring out whether there is a market for digital cash is not impossible,
I'm just not sure if anyone has done it yet.

> This is why I still favor the "get it out there and see who salutes"
> system, where products either succeed or fail. But, again, my bias in
> these comments is toward "technology-driven" high-tech products rather
> than service-driven products.

Of course, this system also results in far more product flops. If you do
a little market research first, produce a prototype, and evaluate that,
you may decide to cancel the product before you've invested much in it
at all.

