Date: Thu, 2 Dec 1993 19:26:17 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Charity for Entrepreneurs

A recurring theme here in Silicon Valley reflects the wannabe
entrepreneur's plea to "buy my dream." The market pays cash for today's
products and services, not something phrased in future tenses.

I prefer the test of the market to the faith of the investor. I know
numerous individuals around here who are the embodiment of Tim May's
characterization of people thinking the world owes them a salary for their
divine insight while they figure out what to do when they grow up (yes, I'm
just as harsh as Tim about these things, if not more so).

Harry Shapiro raises key points about market research, but more often than
not big firms use market research to justify decisions they've already made
and startups don't have prototypes to demo to get useful feedback. Less
than these extremes, however, some startups use market research to identify
areas of vulnerability in competitors' offerings or positioning.

A strategy that appears to be repeatedly successful sees short-term to
market as a way to fund a startup business, then once the business has cash
on hand, to begin the more extensive r&D (little r BIG D) to increase the
revenue bandwidth and ramp up the operation.

Many successful startups also have a multifaceted strategy where it may
offer product X and sell service Y. As long as X and Y are closely related,
the buffeting of market and execution vagaries can be withstood.

Something else that many wannabe startups don't realize: business execution
is very hard work, with REAL DEADLINES, and has a frightfully deterministic
way of keeping score. In the business play, the operational beats the
theoretical, although the academic environment that is the wellspring of
many wannabes typically (not always) sees the reverse.

Fun stuff, indeed.

