Date: Thu, 2 Dec 93 21:43:26 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Charity for Entrepreneurs

Jamie Dinkelacker:
> The market pays cash for today's
> products and services, not something phrased in future tenses.

I agree with many of the sentiments underlying this claim, but 
it goes overboard.  It is literally untrue.  We have oil wildcatters,
futures markets, Disney 100 year bonds, and on and on:
big chunks of the market are dedicated to anticipating the future.
We have biotech startups that take 10+ years to get first product to market, 
more than a dozen wireless startups, 3DO, etc. all getting $millions 
of VC based on expectations of future products and services, not present
products and services.   We have entire business cultures built
around obtaining pork barrel (a corrupting influence to be shunned,
IMHO, but nevertheless a major source of revenue for many people
that is often not based on existing product or serve).  Jamie
has been involved in such ventures, of course, so I'm sure his
above sentence was not meant to be taken literally.  All that
said, I agree the visions and plans should be exposed to the discipline 
of the marketplace, as concrete product or service, as soon as is
reasonable.

"Charityware" is criticized, but then Tim May goes onto site many
successful companies which "ripped off" charityware and turned it
into lucrative business. (eg the technologies for Sun and
Cisco developed at Stanford, the Mac technology developed at
Xerox PARC, etc).  Going through a charity phase looks like a quite good 
strategy, as long as it is clearly labeled "charity" and its corrupting
influence is minimized (ie the principles don't get so used to charity
they lack motivation or culture to go commercial).  Interval Research 
looks like another big charityware house, even though they hope to
make money on patents and the like.  Rich folks are willing to fund
these things, to a certain extent, although it can rarely be
expected that charity will motivate the same kind of technology
that is best for the marketplace. (NASA and SSC are perhaps the
most extreme examples).

How about let's discuss some actual projects folks are working on?
What are Interval Research and PARC working on these days that
we could "rip off"?   What do folks think about multinational
small (and medium-sized) business as a rapidly emerging software 
market niche?  How risky is it to develop based on the expectation that
MIME and Windows can be found or easily installed on millions of
home boxes by the end of '94, or will some other standards emerge for 
delivering multimedia "net storefronts" to the home?  Or will
VT100 reign supreme for some time to come as multimedia gets
lost in a maze of conflicting "standards", display dependencies,
etc?  Is there really much advantage to real-time interaction
over rapidly delivered e-mail?  Will business/business interaction
move from EDI to TCP/IP, or EDI over TCP/IP, and how many businesses
really care about public-funded "Smart Valleys" when they already
have OSI and private IP on T1's criss-crossing the land?  Who
are the big players in these markets?  Who are the important
players?   What projects do we Extropians have in these areas?  
(Jamie's involvement with Internet provider services, my "Internet 
Storefront" projects, various cypherpunk security projects, Derek's 
multimedia mail project, Habitat 2, Ray's list software, etc.)

Perhaps too many questions to tackle here -- but all 
more important, IMHO, then over-generalization about "what makes a 
company succeed", "what makes a company attractive to investors", and 
whatnot.   How quick the product should go to market, how much investors 
will be willing to invest when, how likely the business is to succeed,
and these other abstract things we've been talking about, are highly 
dependent on the business in question.  Let's start with the concrete
stuff, the draw some conclusions, which will be very different for
different kinds of projects.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

