Nick makes many fine point, and is right on the money that my remark:

>> The market pays cash for today's
>> products and services, not something phrased in future tenses.

is not mean to be taken absolutely literally, but there's more to it than this.

In part, the issue here relates to the perceived audience for this thread.
I've been writing to those who are thinking about startups as something
relatively new for them to do, not for the experienced "been there, done
that" crowd.

As Nick points out,
We have oil wildcatters,
>futures markets, Disney 100 year bonds, and on and on:
>big chunks of the market are dedicated to anticipating the future.
>We have biotech startups that take 10+ years to get first product to market, 
>more than a dozen wireless startups, 3DO, etc. all getting $millions 
>of VC based on expectations of future products and services, not present
>products and services.  

These can be parsed apart. For the security instruments, these have a
market value today that allows the investor to cash out (perhaps at a big
loss) but to cash out nevertheless. And 3DO and some of the wireless
startups weren't started by kids without a track record (e.g., Trip
Hawkins). For every startup I see funded, perhaps a dozen or so die on the
vine. Some of these are the dreamers Tim May discussed.

Another element has to do with how much time elapses before the "future"
delivers dollars. A company that realistically projects a return of $10M
from an investment of $1M over a three year span is a good investment. But
this isn't what Tim and I were saying about those who want a salary while
they figure out what to do. Most often, the investment money goes to those
with a good plan, reasonable expections for execution, and an attractive
revenue stream within a finite time period.

And it's no surprise that if the people have a track record in getting
things to market, then VC or other investment money is easier to get than
if they don't. One key element of doing a startup, is knowing how to do it
("been there, done that").


Nick also goes on to suggest:
>
>How about let's discuss some actual projects folks are working on?
>What are Interval Research and PARC working on these days that
>we could "rip off"?   ...   What projects do we Extropians have in these
>areas?  
>(Jamie's involvement with Internet provider services, my "Internet 
>Storefront" projects, various cypherpunk security projects, Derek's 
>multimedia mail project, Habitat 2, Ray's list software, etc.)

Remember: LOOSE LIPS SINK SHIPS.

Yes, my internet connectivity project consists of wiring businesses up with
ISDN, concentrating them into PRIs, making money off the economies of scale
by reselling T1 digital bandwidth, and getting established and ready for
ATM roll-out. This same firm also puts other companies "on the net" by
providing full "server services," giving them their own domain names
(behind our ".net" name), access via ftp, gopher, WAIS, WWW, and list
management, so they appear to the net to be accessible. This I can talk
about, to a limited extent, because of the marketing roll-out in several
days at Internet World in NYC. This die is cast.

But more importantly, its suicide to talk about what I'm working on in the
commercial startup sphere. For example, I'm also working in some measure
for three other companies: one is repositioning an interesting text
processing technology, another is developing sound files for a particular
platform, and the third is developing models for an online marketplace (my
favorite). However, if I were to talk about who these companies are, why
they're doing what they're doing, where their money comes from, their
product/service features, their strategic reasons for going in one
direction or another, their anticipated roll-out dates, or the like, my
name would be mud and I'd never work in this valley again as a marketing
and management consultant. 

Real money is at stake here, and company secrets are not broadcast -- let
alone the somewhat Draconian restrictions of some non-disclosure contracts.
Does Gimbals tell Macys? Of course not! Sorry, but I take my
responsibilities to my clients very seriously. They're my lifeblood and
I'll do nothing, ever, to harm them (or even give the slightest hint of a
perception of harm or risk). Lips are zipped.

If you want concrete stuff, this is as basic as it gets. Commerce means
competition -- success and discreet circumspection go hand in hand.

