Date: Thu, 2 Dec 1993 03:27:00 -0500
From: xiaozhou@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Kate Xiao Zhou)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

Tim May said:

>Face it, copyrights as we understand them today are going the way of
>the once-sacred "ownership" of guild knowledge. No matter how much
>they squawked, the leathermakers guild could not hold their knowledge
>secret once book publishing appeared.

This is obvious only if one concentrates on the forces making it easier to
copy things and ignores other developments that may work in the opposite
direction. It's still an arms race. Nor is it obvious whether we should
cheer the death of the copyright should it occur.

Persons interested in this topic might be interested in an article by Brad
Cox titled " What if there is a silver bullet and the competition gets it
first?" that appeared in the June 1992  Journal of Object-oriented
Programming and/or(?) Dr. Dobb's Journal of Oct 1992.

Cox writes:
>>Dr. Ryoichi Mori, the head of the Japanese industry-wide
>>consortium, JEIDA (Japanese Electronics Industrial
>>Development Association) is developing an analogous approach
>>for software. Each computer is thought of as a station that
>>broadcasts, not the software itself, but the use of the
>>software, to an audience of a single 'listener'[MORI]. The
>>approach is called superdistribution because, like
>>superconductivity, it lets information flow freely, without
>>resistance from copy protection or piracy.

Basically, Mori's and other proposals seek to charge for software by usage
instead of/in addition to by the copy. Hardware changes must occur and
there are potentially hairy privacy issues, but this means the death of
copyright is not inevitable. (Privacy issue could be overcome with a
Chaumian observer and some digicash, but likely won't.)

Even if things continue to move in the direction of the software pirates,
large corporate buyers of software cannot afford to violate copyright
because they are large enough targets to be worth hunting. (Or is the large
corporation dying as well? Hmm.) So long as copyright enforcement is
cost-effective (not necessarily bullet-proof) it will likely continue. A
two-tier system may evolve, similar to many current freeware licenses that
allow unlimited private usage and copying except for profit. Basically,
ignore the small fish (or use them as marketing allies), catch the big
ones.

Somebody (Oops, I forgot) said:
>There's an article in the latest issue of Musician magazine on how
>technology is going to bring down the entertainment industry,  since
>there is no reason artists shouldn't be able to distribute their own
>word digitally via the internet.

Seems more likely the industry will move onto the net. Marketing is still
required, even when distribution costs approach zero. They'll probably
decentralize too, but they'll still be there.

