From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Insulated Tubs

Tim Starr wrote:

> 1) How come the eco-fascists haven't been on to this already, mandating
> insulated tubs?  Do I run the risk of giving them the idea by merely asking
> the question?

Rightly or wrongly, many people associate molded (not moldy...) tubs
with cheapness, prefab, trailers, cheap motels, etc., and so
porcelain tubs are in demand. Even more in demand at the high end are
marble tubs.

Also, most newer tubs I have seen are enclosed (box-like), with some
kind of air space or perhaps even insulation between the tub walls and
the outer walls of the box structure. The "naked porcelain tub"
standing on clawed legs is not too common. Thus, it is possible that
nearly _all_ tubs, porcelain or plastic, are already insulated to some
extent.

We could do some experiments to measure the rate of heat loss. The
specific heat of the tub itself has to be taken into account (the temp
drop, for example, that comes from heating the tub material
itself)...this could be done easily by letting in small amounts of
water at the desired starting temp. until equilibrium is achieved
(e.g., after 30 minutes or so) and then turning off the water and
recording the temp. drop with time. (Placing a layer of bubble-pak
across the surface would reduce heat loss through the surface as an
issue, giving better measurement of the wall loss.)

(After the experiment is over, drain the warm water and add cold water
and see how warm it gets, as a double-check of the specific heat of
the tub calculation.)

My hunch is that wall loss is less than 20% of the heat loss for
conventional plastic tubs and less than 40% of the loss for porcelain
tubs, with surface loss and specific heat lag causing most of the
apparent heat loss. (The bubble pack experiment above will measure the
air loss component, and the calcs already described will allow the
specific heat componenet to be characterized.)

> 2) Insulated hot tubs exist.  A company called "Softub" (I think) exhibited
> portable hot tubs made out of a sort of vinyl-like material that insulated
> them against heat loss through the sides, with a removable cover made out of

This is not news, Tim. _Every_ hot tub I have seen in the last dozen
years, except for the old hippie-style redwood tubs, is heavily
insulated with urethane foam and the like. The lid technology is
similarly advanced. Hot tubs can now be kept at temp all year round
without a direct power line to Diablo Canyon.

Insulation is not as prevalent in bathtubs as in hot tubs for several
good reasons:

- the bathtub is only in use perhaps 30 minutes a day on average (2%
of the time the outdoor hot tub contains water)

- the hot water in the bathtub gets dumped anyway (meaning the small
loss in temperature is a fraction of the "thrown away heat")

- the temperature differential is lower for the bathtub (compared to
the outdoor tub)... heat loss is proportional to (delta-T)^n, where n is
between 2 and 4, so the large delta-T outdoors is especially serious
for convective and radiative heat loss. A lot of insulation will
reduce the delta-T from the hot tub outer surface to the air and sky.

- any heat loss inside the home goes into heating the home in the
evenings and mornings when most baths are taken (which is probably
useful for 7 months out of the year in most parts of the country, a
lose for 3 months, and a wash for 3 months). On average, the heat loss
is not a waste. (Ironically, an efficient tub with no heating of the
tub and no air loss would "waste" more heat by letting it go drain the
drain into the sewer system. Ha, wouldn't this freak out the eco-chic
freaks?)

None of these points is to say that better insulated bath tubs would
not be a good, or saleable thing. A ceramic or porcelain tub with
integeral insulation could be a hot seller. I would not be at all
surprises to find out that luxury tubs are already insulated.

It is interesting and revealing to see that ubermensch Extropians like
ourselves are debating shoes and bath tubs with such vigor.

